</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
Arithmetic in the exponent is taken mod <span
class="math inline">\phi(N)</span>
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize dada-hfill-layout">
<span style="color: black">If</span> <span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">a</span></span> <span style="color: black">has no
divisors in common with</span> <span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">N</span></span><span style="color: black">, then
</span>
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 30%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<div class="center">
<p><span style="color: blue"><span class="math inline">{a^{x}\bmod
N=a^{x\bmod\phi(N)}\bmod N}</span></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<span style="color: black">, so for example</span>
</div>
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span class="roman"> </span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize dada-hfill-layout">
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 50%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">a^{28}\bmod15=a^{28\bmod8}\bmod15=a^{4}\bmod15</span></span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<span style="color: black">.</span>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Quadratic residues<img data-src="../../images/Zp.png"
style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:0.3cm" alt="image" /></h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title block-title-hfill">
<span class="block-title-left">The square root of <span
class="math inline">x\in\mathbb{Z}_{p}</span> is...</span><span
class="block-title-right">(primes)</span>
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">...a number </span><span
style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">y\in\mathbb{Z}_{p}</span></span><span
style="color: black"> such that </span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">y^{2}\equiv x\bmod p</span></span><span
style="color: black">. Note that </span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">\sqrt{2}\mod{\,}7=3</span></span><span
style="color: black">, but </span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">\sqrt{3}\mod{\,}7</span></span><span
style="color: black"> doesn’t exist.</span></p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<div style="color: black">
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 3px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
</div>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{p}^{*}</span></span><span
style="color: black"> is the multiplicative (sub) group and an element
</span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">x\in\mathbb{Z}_{p}^{*}</span></span><span
style="color: black"> is called a </span><span style="color: blue">QR
(Quadratic Residue)</span><span style="color: black"> if its square root
exists in </span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{p}</span></span><span
style="color: black">. Each </span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">x\in\mathbb{Z}_{p}^{*}</span> </span><span
style="color: black">has either </span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">0</span></span><span style="color: black"> or
</span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">2</span></span><span style="color: black"> square
roots.</span></p>
</div>
<ul>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span style="color: black">If
</span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">a</span></span><span style="color: black"> is a
square root of</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">x\mod p</span></span><span style="color: black">,
then so is </span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">-a\mod p</span></span><span
style="color: black">.</span></span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span
style="color: black">Exactly half of the elements in</span><span
style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{p}^{*}</span></span><span
style="color: black"> are QR.</span></span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">a</span></span><span style="color: black"> is a QR
(<span class="math inline">{\color{blue}\bmod p}</span>) iff</span><span
style="color: blue"> <span class="math inline">a^{\frac{p-1}{2}}\bmod
p=1</span></span><span style="color: black"> (Euler’s
criterion).</span></span></li>
</ul>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<div style="color: black">
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 3px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
</div>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">Computing square roots in</span><span
style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{p}^{*}</span></span><span
style="color: black"> is computationally possible. An </span><span
style="color: blue"><span class="math inline">{\cal
O}(n^{4})</span></span><span style="color: black"> randomized algorithm
exists. Easier in some special cases of </span><span
style="color: blue"><span class="math inline">p</span></span><span
style="color: black">, e.g. when</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">p=3\mod 4</span></span>, then</p>
</div>
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span class="roman"> </span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 35%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<div class="center">
<p><span style="color: blue"><span class="math inline">{\sqrt{x}\mod
p=x^{\frac{p+1}{4}}\mod 4}</span></span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">Given an</span><span style="color: blue">
<span class="math inline">x\in\mathbb{Z}_{p}^{*}</span></span><span
style="color: black">, deciding whether </span><span
style="color: blue"><span class="math inline">x</span> </span><span
style="color: black">is a </span><span
style="color: blue">QR</span><span style="color: black"> is
computationally easy.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Identifying quadratic residues<img data-src="../../images/Zp.png"
style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:0.3cm" alt="image" /> <img data-src="../../images/ZN.png"
style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:0.3cm" alt="image" /></h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<div class="image-grid">
<img data-src="../../images/Legendre.jpeg" style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:1.5cm"
alt="image" />
<img data-src="../../images/QRvsNQR.png" style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:1.5cm"
alt="image" />
<img data-src="../../images/Carl_Jacobi.jpeg" style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:1.5cm"
alt="image" />
</div>
</div>
<div class="center">
<p> </p>
</div>
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
The Legendre and Jacobi “symbol” notation
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">Legendre:</span><span style="color: black">
<span class="dada-emph"><span
style="color: black">identifies</span></span> the QR in</span><span
style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{p}</span></span><span
style="color: black">; a function of </span><span
style="color: blue"><span class="math inline">a</span></span><span
style="color: black"> and </span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">p</span></span><span style="color: black">:
</span></p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">(\mathbb{Z}_{p})</span></span></p>
</div>
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span class="roman"> </span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 65%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<div class="center">
<p><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">{\begin{array}{lllll}
\left(\frac{a}{p}\right) &amp; = &amp; a^{\frac{p-1}{2}}\mod p &amp; =
&amp; \left\{ \begin{array}{lll}
1 &amp;  &amp; {\color{black}\mathrm{if}\,a\,\mathrm{is\,a\,QR}}\\
-1 &amp;  &amp; {\color{black}\mathrm{if}\,a\,\mathrm{is\,not\,a\,QR}}\\
0 &amp;  &amp; {\color{blue}{\color{black}\mathrm{if}\,a=0\mod p}}
\end{array}\right.\end{array}}</span></span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">Jacobi:</span> is a generalization of
Legendre, extending it to<span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{N}</span></span> (composites).</p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">(\mathbb{Z}_{N})</span></span><span
style="color: black"> </span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>Easy to compute given it’s prime factorization. For example,
given<span style="color: blue"> <span class="math inline">N=p\times
q</span></span> with<span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">p\neq q</span></span>, then</p>
</div>
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span class="roman"> </span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 35%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<div class="center">
<p><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">{\left(\frac{a}{N}\right)=\left(\frac{a}{p}\right)\left(\frac{a}{q}\right)}</span></span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>Given <span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">a</span></span> and <span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">N</span></span>, we can find the Jacobi symbol
without knowing the factorization of<span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">N</span></span>. From the Jacobi symbol, we can’t
tell whether<span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">a</span> </span> is a QR, but no entry with <span
style="color: blue"><span class="math inline">-1</span></span> is a
quadratic residue.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Outline</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="dada-toc">
<ol>
<li><span style="color: lightgray">Details on ECC</span></li>
<li><span style="color: blue"><span class="dada-bold">Building blocks
for “hardness” operations in <span
class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{p}</span> and <span
class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{N}</span></span> </span>
<ul>
<li><span style="color: lightgray">Cyclic groups, math
operations...</span></li>
<li><span class="dada-bold">Algorithms, with complexity</span></li>
<li><span style="color: lightgray">Problems in <span
class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{p}</span> and <span
class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{N}</span> </span></li>
</ul></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Complexity of basic arithmetic<img data-src="../../images/Zp.png"
style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:0.3cm" alt="image" /> <img data-src="../../images/ZN.png"
style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:0.3cm" alt="image" /></h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
Basic ideas...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">We often work with (cyclic) groups in
</span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{p}</span> </span><span
style="color: black">and</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{N}</span></span><span
style="color: black"> with very large </span><span
style="color: blue"><span class="math inline">p</span></span><span
style="color: black"> and </span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">N</span></span><span style="color: black">.
</span></p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<div style="color: black">
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 3px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
</div>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">Computational complexity: </span><span
style="color: black">the following basic arithmetic operations can be
efficiently computed in </span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{p}</span> </span><span
style="color: black">and </span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{N}</span></span><span
style="color: black">: </span></p>
</div>
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span class="roman"> </span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<div class="dada-fbox dada-font-footnotesize"
style="width: 34%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<div class="table-with-borders">
<table>
<thead>
<tr style="background-color: rgb(0,255,255);">
<th style="text-align: left;"><span class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span
class="dada-bold">Operations</span></span></th>
<th style="text-align: center;"><span
class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span
class="dada-bold">Complexity</span></span></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="spacer"><td></td></tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><span
class="dada-font-footnotesize">Addition</span></td>
<td style="text-align: center;"><span
class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">{\cal O}(m)</span></span></span></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><span
class="dada-font-footnotesize">Multiplication</span></td>
<td style="text-align: center;"><span
class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">{\cal O}(m^{2})</span></span></span></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><span
class="dada-font-footnotesize">Inverse</span></td>
<td style="text-align: center;"><span
class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">{\cal O}(m^{2})</span></span></span></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><span
class="dada-font-footnotesize">Exponentiation</span></td>
<td style="text-align: center;"><span
class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">{\cal O}(m^{3})</span></span></span></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">Programming issues:</span><span
style="color: black"> Since our machines work with 32 or 64-bit
integers, we have libraries such as the GMP (GNU Multiple Precision)
library, and Java “BigInteger” classes. </span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>On finding generators:<img data-src="../../images/Zp.png"
style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:0.3cm" alt="image" /></h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 70%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<div class="dada-font-tiny">
<p><span
class="dada-bold"><code>gens=</code><span><code>[</code></span><span><code>]</code></span></span></p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-tiny">
<p><span class="dada-bold"><code>for s in facs:</code></span></p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-tiny">
<p><span class="dada-bold"><code>g=2</code></span></p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-tiny">
<p><span
class="dada-bold"><code>generators=</code><span><code>[</code></span><span><code>]</code></span></span></p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-tiny">
<p><span
class="dada-bold"><code>while g&lt;s</code><span><code>[</code></span><code>0</code><span><code>]</code></span><code>:</code></span></p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-tiny">
<p><span class="dada-bold"><code>gen=1</code></span></p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-tiny">
<p><span
class="dada-bold"><code>for j in s</code><span><code>[</code></span><code>1</code><span><code>]</code></span><code>:</code></span></p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-tiny">
<p><span
class="dada-bold"><code>if (g</code><span><code>*</code></span><span><code>*</code></span><code>((s</code><span><code>[</code></span><code>0</code><span><code>]</code></span><code>-1)/j))%s</code><span><code>[</code></span><code>0</code><span><code>]</code></span><code>==1:</code></span></p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-tiny">
<p><span class="dada-bold"><code>gen=0</code></span></p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-tiny">
<p><span class="dada-bold"><code>break</code></span></p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-tiny">
<p><span class="dada-bold"><code>if gen:</code></span></p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-tiny">
<p><span class="dada-bold"><code>generators.append(g)</code></span></p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-tiny">
<p><span class="dada-bold"><code>g=g+1</code></span></p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-tiny">
<p><span
class="dada-bold"><code>gens.append(</code><span><code>[</code></span><code>s</code><span><code>[</code></span><code>0</code><span><code>]</code></span><code>,generators</code><span><code>]</code></span><code>)</code></span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="exampleblock">
<div class="block-title">
Algorithm to find a generator modulo <span class="math inline">p</span>:
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<ol>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize">Find prime factors<span
style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">q_{1},q_{2},\ldots</span></span> of<span
style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">\phi(p)</span></span>.</span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize">Select a random candidate<span
style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">a</span></span>.</span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize">For each factor <span
style="color: blue"><span class="math inline">q_{i}</span></span>,
calculate<span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">a^{\frac{p-1}{q_{i}}}\bmod p</span></span>. If any
one is <span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">1</span></span>, then back to step 2.</span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize">Otherwise<span
style="color: blue"> <span class="math inline">a</span></span> is a
generator.</span></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>CRT - the Chinese Remainder Theorem<img
data-src="../../images/ZN.png" style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:0.3cm" alt="image" /></h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 90%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<img data-src="../../images/ChineseEx.png" style="height: auto; width:100.0%"
alt="image" />
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>CRT - the Chinese Remainder Theorem<img
data-src="../../images/ZN.png" style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:0.3cm" alt="image" /></h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="exampleblock">
<div class="block-title">
Worked example
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize dada-hfill-layout">
The original problem:
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 30%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<div class="center">
<p><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">{\begin{array}{ccl}
x &amp; = &amp; 2\,\mathrm{mod}\,3\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,(1)\\
x &amp; = &amp; 3\,\mathrm{mod}\,5\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,(2)\\
x &amp; = &amp; 2\,\mathrm{mod}\,7\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,(3)
\end{array}}</span></span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 3px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>From (1), we know that<span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">x=3n+2</span></span> for some <span
style="color: blue"><span class="math inline">n</span></span>.
Substituting this in (2) gives<span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">3n=1\,\mathrm{mod}\,5</span></span> . This reduces
to a simpler equation<span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">n=2\,\mathrm{mod}\,5</span></span> which is
equivalent to<span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">n=5m+2</span> fo</span> r some<span
style="color: blue"> <span class="math inline">m</span></span>.
Substituting this back into<span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">x=3n+2</span></span> gives us<span
style="color: blue"> <span class="math inline">x=15m+8</span></span>.
Substituting this in (3) gives <span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">15m+8=2\,\mathrm{mod}\,7</span></span>, or<span
style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">m=1\,\mathrm{mod}\,7</span></span>, i.e.<span
style="color: blue"> <span class="math inline">m=7o+1</span></span>.</p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 3px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>From this we can see that<span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">x=105o+23</span></span>. Note that<span
style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">105=\mathrm{lcm}(3,5,7)</span></span> and the
solutions are<span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">23,128</span></span> (and so on).</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Euclidean algorithm<img data-src="../../images/ZN.png"
style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:0.3cm" alt="image" /></h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="exampleblock">
<div class="block-title">
Some theory to compute the multiplicative inverses.
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>To find the <span class="dada-bold"><code>gcd</code></span> of two
numbers (say<span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">2394</span></span> and <span
style="color: blue"><span class="math inline">154</span></span> ), use
the prime factorization:</p>
</div>
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span class="roman"> </span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-font-scriptsize">
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 40%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<div class="center">
<p><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">{\begin{array}{rll}
2394 &amp; = &amp; 2*3*3*7*19\\
154 &amp; = &amp; 2*7*11\\
\therefore\mathrm{gcd}(2394,154) &amp; = &amp; 2*7=14
\end{array}}</span></span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
Euclidean algorithm
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>Unfortunately, it is not easy to find the prime factors of integers.
The <span class="dada-bold"><code>gcd</code></span> of two integers can
however be found by repeated application of division, using the
Euclidean algorithm. You repeatedly divide the divisor by the remainder
until the remainder is 0. The <span
class="dada-bold"><code>gcd</code></span> is the last non-zero
remainder.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="dada-bubble"
style="background-color: rgb(128,255,255); width: 90%; margin-left: auto; margin-right: 0;">
<p>Example:</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Extended Euclidean algorithm<img data-src="../../images/ZN.png"
style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:0.3cm" alt="image" /></h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
An interesting property for inverses
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">If the </span><span
style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">\mathrm{gcd}(a,b)=r</span></span><span
style="color: black"> then there exist integers  </span><span
style="color: blue"> <span class="math inline">m</span></span><span
style="color: black">  and </span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">n</span></span><span style="color: black">  so
that</span><span style="color: blue">  <span
class="math inline">ma+nb=r</span></span><span style="color: black">.
</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="exampleblock">
<div class="block-title">
The extended Euclidean algorithm
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<ol>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span style="color: black">We
begin by dividing </span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">n</span> </span><span
style="color: black"> by </span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">x</span></span><span style="color: black">, and as
we carry out each step </span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">i</span> </span><span style="color: black"> of the
Euclidean algorithm discovering the quotient  </span><span
style="color: blue"><span class="math inline">q_{i}</span></span><span
style="color: black">, we also calculate an extra number  </span><span
style="color: blue"><span class="math inline">x_{i}</span>.</span><span
style="color: black"> For the first two steps </span><span
style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">x_{0}=0</span></span><span style="color: black">
 and </span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">x_{1}=1</span></span><span style="color: black">.
For the following steps, calculate </span><span style="color: blue">
<span
class="math inline">x_{i}=x_{i-2}-x_{i-1}q_{i-2}\,\mathrm{mod}\,n</span></span><span
style="color: black">. </span></span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span style="color: black">If
the last non-zero remainder occurs at step </span><span
style="color: blue"> <span class="math inline">k</span></span><span
style="color: black">, then if it is </span><span style="color: blue">
<span class="math inline">1</span></span><span style="color: black">, 
</span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">x</span></span><span style="color: black">  has an
inverse: </span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">x_{k+2}</span></span><span
style="color: black">.</span></span></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div class="dada-bubble"
style="background-color: rgb(128,255,255); width: 90%; margin-left: auto; margin-right: 0;">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>The inverse of <span class="math inline">15</span> modulo <span
class="math inline">26</span>, showing the method</p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-tiny">
<p><span style="color: blue">:</span></p>
</div>
<p><br />
</p>
<div class="dada-font-tiny">
<p><span style="color: blue"></span></p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 80%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<div class="center">
<p><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">{\begin{array}{llllllll}
0:\,\,\, &amp; 26 &amp; = &amp; 1*15+11 &amp; \,\,\,\,\, &amp; x_{0}=0\\
1:\,\,\, &amp; 15 &amp; = &amp; 1*11+4 &amp;  &amp; x_{1}=1\\
2:\,\,\, &amp; 11 &amp; = &amp; 2*4+3 &amp;  &amp;
x_{2}=0-1*1\,\mathrm{mod}\,26 &amp; = &amp; 25\\
3:\,\,\, &amp; 4 &amp; = &amp; 1*3+1 &amp;  &amp;
x_{3}=1-25*1\,\mathrm{mod}\,26 &amp; = &amp; 2\\
4:\,\,\, &amp; 3 &amp; = &amp; 3*1+0 &amp;  &amp;
x_{4}=25-2*2\,\mathrm{mod}\,26 &amp; = &amp; 21\\
&amp;  &amp;  &amp;  &amp;  &amp; x_{5}=2-21*1\,\mathrm{mod}\,26 &amp; =
&amp; 7
\end{array}}</span></span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Outline</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="dada-toc">
<ol>
<li><span style="color: lightgray">Details on ECC</span></li>
<li><span style="color: blue"><span class="dada-bold">Building blocks
for “hardness” operations in <span
class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{p}</span> and <span
class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{N}</span></span> </span>
<ul>
<li><span style="color: lightgray">Cyclic groups, math
operations...</span></li>
<li><span style="color: lightgray">Algorithms, with
complexity</span></li>
<li><span class="dada-bold">Problems in <span
class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{p}</span> and <span
class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{N}</span></span></li>
</ul></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Easy problems in <span class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{p}</span><img
data-src="../../images/Zp.png" style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:0.3cm" alt="image" /></h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
(...assuming <span class="math inline">p</span> is very large)
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<dl>
<dt><span><span style="color: blue">Basic math:</span></span></dt>
<dd>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">Generating</span><span
style="color: black"> a </span><span
style="color: blue">random</span><span style="color: black"> element,
</span><span style="color: blue">adding</span><span
style="color: black"> and </span><span
style="color: blue">multiplying</span><span style="color: black">
elements, finding </span><span style="color: blue">inverse</span><span
style="color: black">, </span><span
style="color: blue">exponentiation</span><span style="color: black">.
</span></p>
</div>
</dd>
<dt><span><span style="color: blue">QR:</span></span></dt>
<dd>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">Testing</span><span style="color: black">
if an element is a </span><span style="color: blue">QR</span><span
style="color: black"> and computing its </span><span
style="color: blue">square root mod p</span><span style="color: black">
if it is QR. </span></p>
</div>
</dd>
<dt><span><span style="color: blue">DDH:</span></span></dt>
<dd>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">Decisional Diffie-Hellman
problem</span><span style="color: black">: Let</span><span
style="color: blue"> <span class="math inline">g</span></span><span
style="color: black"> be a generator of a cyclic group </span><span
style="color: blue"><span class="math inline">{\cal
G}</span></span><span style="color: black">. Given a positive integer
3-tuple </span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">(a,b,z)</span></span><span style="color: black">,
distinguish with probability greater than </span><span
style="color: blue"><span class="math inline">0.5</span></span><span
style="color: black"> whether the three outputs are from process A or
B:</span></p>
</div>
</dd>
</dl>
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span class="roman"> </span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 45%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<dl>
<dt><span><span style="color: blue">A:</span></span></dt>
<dd>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">Output <span
class="math inline">(g^{a},g^{b},g^{z})</span></span></p>
</div>
</dd>
<dt><span><span style="color: blue">B:</span></span></dt>
<dd>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">Output <span
class="math inline">(g^{a},g^{b},g^{ab})</span></span></p>
</div>
</dd>
</dl>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">For the group</span><span
style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{p}^{*}</span></span><span
style="color: black">, DDH is not hard (...use the Jacobi
symbol).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Believed to be hard problems in <span
class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{p}</span><img
data-src="../../images/Zp.png" style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:0.3cm" alt="image" /></h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
But anything can change...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<dl>
<dt><span><span style="color: blue">DL:</span></span></dt>
<dd>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">Discrete log problem: </span><span
style="color: black">Let</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">g</span></span><span style="color: black"> be a
generator of a cyclic group. Given</span><span style="color: blue">
<span class="math inline">x</span></span><span style="color: black">,
find</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">r</span></span><span style="color: black"> such
that</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">x=g^{r}</span></span><span
style="color: black">.</span></p>
</div>
</dd>
<dt><span><span style="color: blue">CDH:</span></span></dt>
<dd>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">Computational Diffie-Hellman problem:
</span><span style="color: black">Let</span><span style="color: blue">
<span class="math inline">g</span></span><span style="color: black"> be
a generator of a cyclic group. Given</span><span style="color: blue">
<span class="math inline">g^{a}</span></span><span
style="color: black">, </span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">g^{b}</span></span><span style="color: black">, find
</span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">g^{ab}</span></span><span style="color: black">.
</span></p>
</div>
</dd>
</dl>
</div>
</div>
<div class="exampleblock">
<div class="block-title">
Cryptosystems based on the hardness of these problems:
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<ol>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span
style="color: blue">Diffie-Hellman</span><span style="color: black"> key
exchange</span></span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span
style="color: blue">Elgamal</span><span style="color: black"> public key
encryption</span></span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span
style="color: black">DL-based digital signatures like </span><span
style="color: blue">Elgamal</span><span style="color: black">,
</span><span style="color: blue">DSA</span><span style="color: black">,
</span><span style="color: blue">DSS</span><span
style="color: black">...</span></span></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Easy problems in <span class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{N}</span><img
data-src="../../images/ZN.png" style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:0.3cm" alt="image" /></h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
(...assuming <span class="math inline">N=p\times q</span>, and <span
class="math inline">p,q</span> are large primes)
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<dl>
<dt><span><span style="color: blue">Basic math:</span></span></dt>
<dd>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">Generating</span><span
style="color: black"> a </span><span
style="color: blue">random</span><span style="color: black"> element,
</span><span style="color: blue">adding</span><span
style="color: black"> and </span><span
style="color: blue">multiplying</span><span style="color: black">
elements, finding </span><span style="color: blue">inverse</span><span
style="color: black">, </span><span
style="color: blue">exponentiation</span><span
style="color: black">.</span></p>
</div>
</dd>
<dt><span><span style="color: blue">Jacobi:</span></span></dt>
<dd>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">Finding</span><span style="color: black">
the </span><span style="color: blue">Jacobi</span><span
style="color: black"> symbol.</span></p>
</div>
</dd>
</dl>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Hard problems in <span class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{N}</span>
<img data-src="../../images/ZN.png" style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:0.3cm"
alt="image" /></h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
These get easier if factorization is known...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<dl>
<dt><span><span style="color: blue">QR:</span></span></dt>
<dd>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">Testing</span><span style="color: black">
if an element is a </span><span style="color: blue">QR</span><span
style="color: black"> in</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{N}</span></span><span
style="color: black">. </span></p>
</div>
<p><br />
</p>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">(Computing the Jacobi symbol cannot solve
the problem).</span></p>
</div>
</dd>
<dt><span><span style="color: blue">SQRT:</span></span></dt>
<dd>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">Computing</span><span style="color: black">
the </span><span style="color: blue">square root</span><span
style="color: black"> of a QR in</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{N}</span></span><span
style="color: black">. </span></p>
</div>
<p><br />
</p>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">Provably as hard as factoring </span><span
style="color: blue"><span class="math inline">N</span></span><span
style="color: black">. </span></p>
</div>
<p><br />
</p>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">Rabin cryptosystems based on this
hardness.</span></p>
</div>
</dd>
<dt><span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">\phi(N)</span>:</span></span></dt>
<dd>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">Computing <span
class="math inline">\phi(N)</span></span><span style="color: black"> -
Provably as hard as factoring</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">N</span></span><span style="color: black">. If you
can efficiently compute </span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">\phi(N)</span></span><span style="color: black">,
then you can also factor</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">N</span></span><span style="color: black">.
</span></p>
</div>
</dd>
<dt><span><span style="color: blue">Roots:</span></span></dt>
<dd>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">Computing the e-th roots mod <span
class="math inline">N</span> </span><span style="color: black">when
</span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">gcd(e,N)=1</span></span><span style="color: black">.
</span></p>
</div>
<p><br />
</p>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">RSA cryptosystems based on this hardness.
</span></p>
</div>
</dd>
</dl>
</div>
</div>
<div class="alertblock">
<div class="block-title">
These do not get easier if factorization is known...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<dl>
<dt><span><span style="color: blue">DL:</span></span></dt>
<dd>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">Discrete log problem</span><span
style="color: black"> With </span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">g</span> </span><span style="color: black">being a
generator of</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{N}^{*}</span></span><span
style="color: black">, then </span></p>
</div>
<p><br />
</p>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">given</span><span style="color: blue">
<span class="math inline">x\in\mathbb{Z}_{N}^{*}</span></span><span
style="color: black">, find an </span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">r</span> </span><span style="color: black">such
that</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">x=g^{r}\bmod N</span></span><span
style="color: black">.</span></p>
</div>
</dd>
<dt><span><span style="color: blue">CDH:</span></span></dt>
<dd>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">Computational Diffie-Hellman
problem</span><span style="color: black"> With </span><span
style="color: blue"><span class="math inline">g</span> </span><span
style="color: black">being a generator of</span><span
style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{N}^{*}</span></span><span
style="color: black">, then given</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">g^{a},g^{b}\in\mathbb{Z}_{N}^{*}</span></span><span
style="color: black">, find</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">g^{ab}\bmod N</span></span><span
style="color: black">.</span></p>
</div>
</dd>
</dl>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Bilinear maps/pairings</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<p><img data-src="../../images/BilinearMap.png" style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:3.5cm"
alt="image" /></p>
</div>
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
Pairings...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>A (symmetric style) bilinear map is a pairing between two elements of
a group to a second (same order) group:<span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">G\times G\rightarrow G_{T}</span></span>. There are
also (asymmetric) pairings like<span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">G_{1}\times G_{2}\rightarrow
G_{T}</span></span>.</p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 3px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>Such a symmetric mapping (if it can be efficiently computed) can be
used to solve DDH:<span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">z=ab</span></span> iff<span style="color: blue">
<span class="math inline">e(g,g^{z})=e(g^{a},g^{b})</span></span>.
Originally this was the focus of bilinear maps in crypto, and this is
why CDH is considered <span class="dada-emph">harder</span> than
DDH.</p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 3px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>The other thing is that it can allow you to reduce the discrete log
problem in <span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">G</span></span> to a (perhaps simpler) discrete log
problem in<span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">G_{T}</span></span>: <span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">e(g,g^{a})=e(g,g)^{a}</span></span>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>A little more on bilinear maps/pairings</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
Can we have “simple” pairings?
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>This is easy, but unfortunately such "simple" maps are not useful for
crypto. A simple map based on (say) integers might be the additive
integer group <span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">G=\left\langle
\mathbb{Z},+\right\rangle</span></span>, and <span
style="color: blue"><span class="math inline">G_{T}=\left\langle
\mathbb{Z},*\right\rangle</span></span>, with</p>
</div>
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span class="roman"> </span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 25%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<div class="center">
<p><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">{e(u,v)=2^{uv}}</span></span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>For the additive group,<span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">u^{2}</span></span> means <span
style="color: blue"><span class="math inline">u+u</span></span>, and it
is sufficient to show that<span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">e(u+u,v)=e(u,v)^{2}</span></span> and<span
style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">e(u,v+v)=e(u,v)^{2}</span></span>. Lets try an
example:</p>
</div>
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span class="roman"> </span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize dada-hfill-layout">
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 25%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<div class="center">
<p><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">{e(3,4)=2^{12}}</span></span></p>
</div>
</div>
and<span style="color: blue"> </span>
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 35%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<div class="center">
<p><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">{\begin{array}{lll}
e(3+3,4) &amp; = &amp; e(3,4)^{2}\\
&amp; = &amp; e(3,4)\times e(3,4)\\
&amp; = &amp; 2^{12}\times2^{12}\\
&amp; = &amp; 2^{24}\\
&amp; = &amp; e(3,4+4)
\end{array}}</span></span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>A little more on bilinear maps/pairings</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 65%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<table>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: center;"><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">G\rightarrow</span></span></td>
<td style="text-align: center;"><img data-src="../../images/Z_5.png"
style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:1.9cm" alt="image" /></td>
<td style="text-align: center;"></td>
<td style="text-align: center;"><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">G_{T}\rightarrow</span></span></td>
<td style="text-align: center;"><img
data-src="../../images/PartZ_11.png" style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:1.9cm"
alt="image" /></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
“Simple” pairings with finite groups?
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>The groups<span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">G=\mathbb{Z}_{5}^{+}</span></span> and <span
style="color: blue"><span class="math inline">G_{T}=\left\langle
3\right\rangle _{11}</span></span>, with <span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">e(u,v)=3^{uv}(\bmod11)</span></span>:</p>
</div>
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span class="roman"> </span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize dada-hfill-layout">
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 35%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<div class="center">
<p><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">{\begin{array}{lll}
e(4,3) &amp; = &amp; 3^{12}\bmod11\\
&amp; = &amp; 9
\end{array}}</span></span></p>
</div>
</div>
and<span style="color: blue"> </span>
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 35%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<div class="center">
<p><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">{\begin{array}{lll}
e(4+4,3) &amp; = &amp; e(4,3)^{2}\\
&amp; = &amp; e(4,3)\times e(4,3)\\
&amp; = &amp; 3^{24}\bmod11\\
&amp; = &amp; 4\\
&amp; = &amp; e(4,3+3)
\end{array}}</span></span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>This is not too useful because groups like this tend to be
invertible.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>The big picture..</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 40%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<img data-src="../../images/OWFtoiO.png" style="height: auto; width:100.0%"
alt="image" />
</div>
</div>
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
In practice? <span class="math inline">G</span> is an elliptic curve,
<span class="math inline">G_{T}</span> is a finite field...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>There are getting to be more uses of pairing based crypto, including
IBE, simple encryption, signatures, and so on. Note that</p>
</div>
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span class="roman"> </span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<div class="dada-fbox"
style="width: 35%; border: 2px solid black; background-color: white; box-sizing: border-box;">
<div class="center">
<p><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">{e(u^{a},v^{b})=e(u^{b},v^{a})}</span></span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>(one for encryption, one for decryption).</p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 3px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>In a talk by Boneh, the above diagram showed crypto history, from
one-way functions, discrete logs, pairings, LWE (Learning with Errors),
through to multilinear maps for indistinguishability obfuscation.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Presented as <span class="dada-emph">one-way</span> functions<img
data-src="../../images/Zp.png" style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:0.3cm" alt="image" /> <img
data-src="../../images/ZN.png" style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:0.3cm" alt="image" /></h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="exampleblock">
<div class="block-title">
Three examples. Assume <span class="math inline">N=p\times q</span> (two
large primes)
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<dl>
<dt><span><span style="color: blue">DL:</span></span></dt>
<dd>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">Discrete Logarithm: </span><span
style="color: black">With prime</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">p</span></span><span style="color: black"> and an
element </span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">g\in\mathbb{Z}_{p}^{*}</span></span><span
style="color: black"> of large order,</span><span style="color: blue">
<span class="math inline">f(x)=g^{x}\bmod p</span></span><span
style="color: black">. It is linear: Given</span><span
style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">a\in\mathbb{Z}</span></span><span
style="color: black">, </span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">f(x)</span></span><span style="color: black">
and</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">f(y)</span></span><span style="color: black">, it is
easy to compute </span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">f(ax)</span></span><span style="color: black">
and</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">f(x+y)</span></span><span style="color: black">. Its
one-wayness is essential for the </span><span
style="color: blue">Diffie-Hellman key exchange</span><span
style="color: black"> protocol and</span><span style="color: blue">
Elgamal public key</span><span style="color: black"> systems.
</span></p>
</div>
</dd>
<dt><span><span style="color: blue">RSA:</span></span></dt>
<dd>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">Let</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">e</span> </span><span style="color: black">be an
integer such that </span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">gcd(e,N)=1</span></span><span
style="color: black">,</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">f(x)=x^{e}\bmod N</span></span><span
style="color: black">. Note that given the factorization of</span><span
style="color: blue"> <span class="math inline">N</span></span><span
style="color: black">, the function</span><span style="color: blue">
<span class="math inline">f</span> </span><span style="color: black">can
be inverted efficiently. The one-wayness property is essential for the
</span><span style="color: blue">RSA</span><span style="color: black">
public key system.</span></p>
</div>
</dd>
<dt><span><span style="color: blue">Rabin:</span></span></dt>
<dd>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue"><span class="math inline">f(x)=x^{2}\bmod
N</span></span><span style="color: black"> This function is one-way if
there is no efficient algorithm to factor</span><span
style="color: blue"> <span class="math inline">N</span></span><span
style="color: black">. The function can be inverted efficiently if the
factorization of</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">N</span> </span><span style="color: black">is known.
The one-wayness property is essential for</span><span
style="color: blue"> Rabin’s scheme</span><span style="color: black">.
</span></p>
</div>
</dd>
</dl>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Summary<img data-src="../../images/Zp.png" style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:0.3cm"
alt="image" /> <img data-src="../../images/ZN.png" style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:0.3cm"
alt="image" /></h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
The framework for “simple” asymmetric systems...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<ul>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span
style="color: black">Multiplicative inverses may not exist in modulo
arithmetic. For a modulus </span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">N</span></span><span style="color: black"> and a
number </span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">x</span></span><span
style="color: black">,</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">x^{-1}</span></span><span style="color: black">
exists iff </span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">gcd(x,N)=1</span></span><span
style="color: black">.</span></span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span style="color: black">CRT
gives a mapping from</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{N}</span></span><span
style="color: black"> to</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{p}\times\mathbb{Z}_{q}</span></span><span
style="color: black">. </span></span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span style="color: black">Some
problems are easy in</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{p}</span> </span><span
style="color: black">but the same problems become difficult
in</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">\mathbb{Z}_{N}</span></span><span
style="color: black"> if the factorization of</span><span
style="color: blue"> <span class="math inline">N</span></span><span
style="color: black"> is unknown.</span></span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span style="color: black">Some
problems, in particular </span><span style="color: blue">Discrete
Log</span><span style="color: black">, are hard in
both.</span></span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span
style="color: blue">Factorization</span><span style="color: black"> and
</span><span style="color: blue">Discrete Log</span><span
style="color: black"> are the two main types of problems. Many
cryptosystems depend on their “hardness”. </span></span></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section></section>
    </div>
  </div>

  <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/reveal.js@4/dist/reveal.js"></script>

  <!-- reveal.js plugins -->
  <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/reveal.js@4/plugin/notes/notes.js"></script>
  <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/reveal.js@4/plugin/search/search.js"></script>
  <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/reveal.js@4/plugin/zoom/zoom.js"></script>

  <script>

      // Full list of configuration options available at:
      // https://revealjs.com/config/
      Reveal.initialize({
        // Display controls in the bottom right corner
        controls: true,

        // Help the user learn the controls by providing hints, for example by
        // bouncing the down arrow when they first encounter a vertical slide
        controlsTutorial: true,

        // Determines where controls appear, "edges" or "bottom-right"
        controlsLayout: 'bottom-right',

        // Visibility rule for backwards navigation arrows; "faded", "hidden"
        // or "visible"
        controlsBackArrows: 'faded',

        // Display a presentation progress bar
        progress: true,

        // Display the page number of the current slide
        slideNumber: true,

        // 'all', 'print', or 'speaker'
        showSlideNumber: 'all',

        // Add the current slide number to the URL hash so that reloading the
        // page/copying the URL will return you to the same slide
        hash: true,

        // Start with 1 for the hash rather than 0
        hashOneBasedIndex: false,

        // Flags if we should monitor the hash and change slides accordingly
        respondToHashChanges: true,

        // Push each slide change to the browser history
        history: false,

        // Enable keyboard shortcuts for navigation
        keyboard: true,

        // Enable the slide overview mode
        overview: true,

        // Disables the default reveal.js slide layout (scaling and centering)
        // so that you can use custom CSS layout
        disableLayout: false,

        // Vertical centering of slides
        center: true,

        // Enables touch navigation on devices with touch input
        touch: true,

        // Loop the presentation
        loop: false,

        // Change the presentation direction to be RTL
        rtl: false,

        // see https://revealjs.com/vertical-slides/#navigation-mode
        navigationMode: 'default',

        // Randomizes the order of slides each time the presentation loads
        shuffle: false,

        // Turns fragments on and off globally
        fragments: true,

        // Flags whether to include the current fragment in the URL,
        // so that reloading brings you to the same fragment position
        fragmentInURL: true,

        // Flags if the presentation is running in an embedded mode,
        // i.e. contained within a limited portion of the screen
        embedded: false,

        // Flags if we should show a help overlay when the questionmark
        // key is pressed
        help: true,

        // Flags if it should be possible to pause the presentation (blackout)
        pause: true,

        // Flags if speaker notes should be visible to all viewers
        showNotes: false,

        // Global override for autoplaying embedded media (null/true/false)
        autoPlayMedia: null,

        // Global override for preloading lazy-loaded iframes (null/true/false)
        preloadIframes: null,

        // Number of milliseconds between automatically proceeding to the
        // next slide, disabled when set to 0, this value can be overwritten
        // by using a data-autoslide attribute on your slides
        autoSlide: 0,

        // Stop auto-sliding after user input
        autoSlideStoppable: true,

        // Use this method for navigation when auto-sliding
        autoSlideMethod: null,

        // Specify the average time in seconds that you think you will spend
        // presenting each slide. This is used to show a pacing timer in the
        // speaker view
        defaultTiming: null,

        // Enable slide navigation via mouse wheel
        mouseWheel: false,

        // The display mode that will be used to show slides
        display: 'block',

        // Hide cursor if inactive
        hideInactiveCursor: true,

        // Time before the cursor is hidden (in ms)
        hideCursorTime: 5000,

        // Opens links in an iframe preview overlay
        previewLinks: false,

        // Transition style (none/fade/slide/convex/concave/zoom)
        transition: 'slide',

        // Transition speed (default/fast/slow)
        transitionSpeed: 'default',

        // Transition style for full page slide backgrounds
        // (none/fade/slide/convex/concave/zoom)
        backgroundTransition: 'fade',

        // Number of slides away from the current that are visible
        viewDistance: 3,

        // Number of slides away from the current that are visible on mobile
        // devices. It is advisable to set this to a lower number than
        // viewDistance in order to save resources.
        mobileViewDistance: 2,

        // The "normal" size of the presentation, aspect ratio will be preserved
        // when the presentation is scaled to fit different resolutions. Can be
        // specified using percentage units.
        width: 1920,

        height: 1080,

        // reveal.js plugins
        plugins: [
          RevealNotes,
          RevealSearch,
          RevealZoom
        ]
      });
    </script>
    </body>
</html>
